{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2520 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "84b0da21d8b9b9cba7f09c61c76a531d75c46c559b503867200625e3f9a0821a" :edb/db-before-t 2519 :edb/db-after-t 2520 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535832 50 #inst "2026-10-09T08:25:59.460Z" 13194139535832 true] [17592186133793 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368848 13194139535832 true] [17592186133793 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368855 13194139535832 true] [17592186133795 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368868 13194139535832 true] [17592186133795 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368875 13194139535832 true] [17592186134481 1017 #edb/ref 17592186133328 13194139535832 false] [17592186134481 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368862 13194139535832 true] [17592186134481 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535832 true] [17592186134481 1100 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,{f}_{3}(t)=t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139535832 true] [17592186134481 1100 "Consider the following set of functions:\n$f_{1}(t) = \\cos t, f_{2}(t) = \\sin (2t), f_{3}(t) = t$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0, π]$ of continuous functions over $[0, π]$ is defined as\n$⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = ∫_{0}^{π}f(t)g(t)dt$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f_{1}(t)⊥f_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n2. $f_{1}(t)⊥f_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n3. $S = {f_{1}(t), f_{2}(t), f_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139535832 false] [17592186134481 1145 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,t⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =0+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368848 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368849 13194139535832 true] [17592186368848 1089 "q-100091" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368848 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535832 true] [17592186368848 1100 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368848 1113 #uuid "07957b89-f6f1-537f-ad10-787ac05c390f" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368848 1145 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-1,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1-1)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(1-1)\\cdot (1+1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(2){|}_{1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(2)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(2)\\cdot (1+1) \\\\  & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368849 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368850 13194139535832 true] [17592186368849 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368851 13194139535832 true] [17592186368849 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368852 13194139535832 true] [17592186368849 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368853 13194139535832 true] [17592186368849 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368854 13194139535832 true] [17592186368849 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535832 true] [17592186368849 1041 "selection" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368849 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368851 13194139535832 true] [17592186368849 1110 #uuid "bd4a38bf-1f11-59a4-a2b9-6037f8331898" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368850 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368850 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368850 1111 #uuid "f3edf2dc-e079-57e5-96d2-8bce38a9f836" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368851 1034 "I only" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368851 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368851 1111 #uuid "1385ec7d-713a-55c8-92da-323cdfcb2ce7" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368852 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368852 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368852 1111 #uuid "2a17dbb6-9043-5b4d-92cb-700ffcbb8d7d" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368853 1034 "None of the statements is true" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368853 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368853 1111 #uuid "612a3ec9-d348-55f6-83af-9b4271e4b630" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368854 1034 "II only" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368854 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368854 1111 #uuid "1dc2081b-9590-5720-b802-00c42e516f92" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368855 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368856 13194139535832 true] [17592186368855 1089 "q-100119" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368855 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535832 true] [17592186368855 1100 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-2,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t+2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2t+4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-2)q(-2)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368855 1113 #uuid "98763a63-2338-5cf6-99f3-477072c178b9" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368855 1145 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,t+2⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (t+2){|}_{-2}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t+2){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2-2)\\cdot ((-2)+2)+(2-2)\\cdot (2+2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,2t+4⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{-2}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2-2)\\cdot (2(-2)+4)+(2-2)\\cdot (2(2)+4) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t+2,2t+4⟩ \\\\  & =(t+2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{-2}+(t+2){|}_{2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{2} \\\\  & =((-2)+2)\\cdot (2(-2)+4)+(2+2)\\cdot (2(2)+4) \\\\  & =32\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368856 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368857 13194139535832 true] [17592186368856 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368858 13194139535832 true] [17592186368856 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368859 13194139535832 true] [17592186368856 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368860 13194139535832 true] [17592186368856 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368861 13194139535832 true] [17592186368856 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535832 true] [17592186368856 1041 "selection" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368856 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368859 13194139535832 true] [17592186368856 1110 #uuid "5546d092-75d2-5847-909d-f560bed58c1a" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368857 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368857 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368857 1111 #uuid "5e388b22-5e8d-5396-ae32-978dd6ad14ea" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368858 1034 "I only" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368858 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368858 1111 #uuid "adc2afe7-960c-51bc-aefd-178846c7b056" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368859 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368859 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368859 1111 #uuid "54bdfaeb-dce4-5299-8d37-57c62e3f1c90" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368860 1034 "None of the statements is true" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368860 1103 #edb/ref 1