368868 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368869 13194139535832 true] [17592186368868 1089 "q-99827" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368868 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535832 true] [17592186368868 1100 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=1,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0$*" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368868 1113 #uuid "cf274c2e-03ff-5221-83a7-3ad5b6f8168e" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368868 1145 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368869 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368870 13194139535832 true] [17592186368869 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368871 13194139535832 true] [17592186368869 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368872 13194139535832 true] [17592186368869 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368873 13194139535832 true] [17592186368869 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368874 13194139535832 true] [17592186368869 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535832 true] [17592186368869 1041 "selection" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368869 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368871 13194139535832 true] [17592186368869 1110 #uuid "e0197e2d-78e4-59b1-866d-0326af420662" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368870 1034 "None of the statements are true" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368870 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368870 1111 #uuid "4116bad4-7704-5d3c-aead-f4e88e539e60" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368871 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368871 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368871 1111 #uuid "d46e0d50-1dfa-5ae7-a1f4-0e084a87d49a" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368872 1034 "I only" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368872 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368872 1111 #uuid "c7817704-2c2c-5c82-b726-44f05a57b617" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368873 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368873 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368873 1111 #uuid "03deeae6-3174-507a-874c-fa238c78bfd9" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368874 1034 "II only" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368874 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368874 1111 #uuid "600d6f02-be35-5d88-88d5-edbe61169d3f" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368875 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368876 13194139535832 true] [17592186368875 1089 "q-99848" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368875 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535832 true] [17592186368875 1100 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{f}_{3}(t)=t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-2,2]$ of continuous functions over $[-2,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368875 1113 #uuid "14911b6b-4e1d-55bf-aff2-fa02f5f045e8" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368875 1145 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-(\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}t\\cdot (t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{2}-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{5}){|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{2}^{5}-(\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}(-2)^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368876 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368877 13194139535832 true] [17592186368876 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368878 13194139535832 true] [17592186368876 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368879 13194139535832 true] [17592186368876 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368880 13194139535832 true] [17592186368876 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368881 13194139535832 true] [17592186368876 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535832 true] [17592186368876 1041 "selection" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368876 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368878 13194139535832 true] [17592186368876 1110 #uuid "c1ca5951-ba3c-5cda-bd2b-1de4dbbfd8d4" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368877 1034 "None of the statements are true" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368877 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368877 1111 #uuid "c87e8ada-6876-5f3b-aef3-f4132922cfb0" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368878 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368878 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368878 1111 #uuid "532611c8-25ed-5d0e-b24b-ff6b277154b3" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368879 1034 "I only" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368879 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368879 1111 #uuid "e085f337-a139-5495-a885-f22a8b50d1d2" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368880 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368880 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368880 1111 #uuid "bbaa87ef-d443-57f2-b170-104896a37179" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368881 1034 "II only" 13194139535832 true] [17592186368881 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535832 true] [17592186368881 1111 #uuid "d15a36c7-be51-5acc-aa89-16a7cb924587" 13194139535832 true]] :edb/temp